    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2897, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S6690\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the public authorities law, in relation to the\ndisposal of real property to cure encroachments\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend the Public Authorities Law (\"PAL\") in relation\nto the disposal of canal real property to cure encroachments.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend PAL §2897(6) by adding a new\nparagraph (e) to permit the Canal Corporation (\"Corporation\") to\ndispose of real property by negotiated sale rather than public\nauction provided that all of the following criteria are met: (i) the\nCorporation determines that the property is no longer necessary or\nuseful and that disposal is in the best interest of the Corporation;\n(ii) the fair market value of the property is greater than $15,000 but\nnot greater than $75,000; (iii) the property was improved before\nApril 1, 1992 (when the Corporation was under the jurisdiction of the\nDepartment of Transportation (\"DOT\")) or improved ·pursuant to a\npermit issued under Canal Law § 100, thereby creating an encroachment;\n(iv) the purchaser is or will be the owner of the encroachment; and\n(v) the purchase price will be the fair market value of the property,\nnot including the value of the encroachment.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nPAL § 2897(6) requires public advertising for bids for property with a\nfair market value that exceeds $15,000 except in certain situations.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nThis bill would create a narrowly tailored exception to the general\nrequirement that the Corporation's lands must be sold through public\nauction. Before coming under the jurisdiction of the New York State\nThruway Authority, the Corporation was under the jurisdiction of DOT.\nDOT could rely on Public Lands Law § 50 which permits the Office of\nGeneral Services to sell abandoned canal lands by private sale (with\nno limitation on value). This bill would give the Corporation the\nauthority to dispose of burdened property using the same basic\napproach but with the limitation that such parcels must be valued at\nno more than $75,000.\n\nThe Corporation has encountered situations where individuals or\nbusinesses have occupied and improved land belonging to the\n\n\nCorporation, either inadvertently or pursuant to a permit granted\nunder Canal Law § 100. It receives numerous requests each year to\nsell small parcels to the owners of such improvements who want to\nclear title to their property. If the value of the desired parcel\nexceeds $ 15,000, the Corporation is required to advertise the\navailability of such property and seek bids. Such a process is often\na wasteful use of resources, because such property is unlikely to\nhave any other interested bidders.\n\nSome owners have asked if they may purchase the smallest portion of\ncanal property necessary to own the land under their encroachment as\na way to stay below the $ 15,000 threshold for public bidding. But\nsuch transactions could leave the Corporation with irregular and\nunmarketable parcels for which it would remain responsible and\nliable, and would therefore not be in the best interest of the\nCorporation.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThe bill has an immediate effective date.\n\n",
