    "lawCode": "Amd §2897, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S6690A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the public authorities law, in relation to the\ndisposal of real property to cure encroachments\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\nThis bill would amend the Public Authorities Law\n(\"PAL\") in relation to the disposal of canal real property to cure\nencroachments.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 of the bill would amend PAL §2897(6)\nby adding a new paragraph (e) to permit the Canal Corporation\n(\"Corporation\") to dispose of real property by negotiated sale rather\nthan public auction provided that all of the following criteria are\nmet: (i) the Corporation determines that the property is no longer\nnecessary or useful and that disposal is in the best interest of the\nCorporation; (ii) an appraisal of the fair market value has been made\nby an independent appraiser and included in the record of the\ntransaction; (iii) the fair market value of the property is greater\nthan $15,000 but not greater than $75,000; (iv) the property was\nimproved before April 1, 1992 under a municipal permit or a permit\nissued under Canal Law § 100, thereby creating an encroachment; (v)\nthe purchaser is or will be the owner of the improvement that either\nfully or partially encroaches; and (vi) the purchase price will be\nnot less than the fair market value of the property, not including the\nvalue of the encroachment.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\nPAL § 2897(6) requires public advertising for bids for\nproperty with a fair market value that exceeds $15,000 except in\ncertain situations.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nThis is a new bill.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\nThis bill would create a narrowly tailored\nexception to the general requirement that the Corporation's lands\nmust be sold through public auction. Before coming under the\njurisdiction of the New York State Thruway Authority, the Corporation\nwas under the jurisdiction of DOT. DOT could rely on Public Lands Law\n\n§ 50 which permits the Office of General Services to sell abandoned\ncanal lands by private sale (with no limitation on value). This bill\nwould give the Corporation the authority to dispose of burdened\n\nproperty using the same basic approach but with the limitation that\nsuch parcels must be valued at no more than $75,000.\n\nThe Corporation has encountered situations where individuals or\nbusinesses have occupied and improved land belonging to the\nCorporation, either inadvertently or pursuant to a permit granted\nunder Canal Law § 100. It receives numerous requests each year to\nsell small parcels to the owners of such improvements who want to\nclear title to their property. If the value of the desired parcel\nexceeds $ 15,000, the Corporation is required to advertise the\navailability of such property and seek bids. Such a process is often\na wasteful use of resources, because such property is unlikely to\nhave any other interested bidders.\n\nSome owners have asked if they may purchase the smallest portion of\ncanal property necessary to own the land under their encroachment as\na way to stay below the $ 15,000 threshold for public bidding. But\nsuch transactions could leave the Corporation with irregular and\nunmarketable parcels for which it would remain responsible and\nliable, and would therefore not be in the best interest of the\nCorporation.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone to State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThe bill has an immediate effective date.\n\n",
